As for the PHP code to compute the distance between two stars based on their coordinates, this requires some complex mathematical calculations and astronomical principles. Here's an example code that uses the Haversine formula to calculate the distance between two stars in light-years:
```
function distance_between_stars($star1_lat, $star1_long, $star2_lat, $star2_long) {
  $distance = 0;
  $distance_constant = 3.262; // number of light-years in a parsec
  $earth_radius = 3960; // radius of Earth in miles
  $d_lat = deg2rad($star2_lat - $star1_lat);
  $d_long = deg2rad($star2_long - $star1_long);
  $a = sin($d_lat/2) * sin($d_lat/2) + cos(deg2rad($star1_lat)) * cos(deg2rad($star2_lat)) * sin($d_long/2) * sin($d_long/2);
  $c = 2 * atan2(sqrt($a), sqrt(1-$a));
  $distance = $earth_radius * $c * $distance_constant;
  return $distance;
}
```
This code takes in the latitude and longitude coordinates of two stars and uses the Haversine formula to calculate the distance between them in miles. It then converts this distance to light-years using a constant value. Note that this code assumes a spherical Earth and uses degrees as the unit of measurement for latitude and longitude.

