To implement a metaphor analyzer in C#, you can use Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques to identify metaphors in a sentence. One way to do this is by using a pre-trained NLP model, such as the Stanford CoreNLP library. 
Here's an example of C# code that uses the Stanford CoreNLP library to analyze the metaphorical sentence "Life is a journey, not a destination":
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline;
using edu.stanford.nlp.ling;
using java.util;
using java.io;
namespace MetaphorAnalyzer
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Set up the Stanford CoreNLP pipeline
            var props = new Properties();
            props.setProperty("annotators", "tokenize, ssplit, pos, lemma, parse, sentiment");
            var pipeline = new StanfordCoreNLP(props);
            // Define the sentence to analyze
            var sentence = "Life is a journey, not a destination.";
            // Create an Annotation object to hold the results of the analysis
            var annotation = new Annotation(sentence);
            // Run the Stanford CoreNLP pipeline on the sentence
            pipeline.annotate(annotation);
            // Get the parse tree for the sentence
            var tree = annotation.get(typeof(edu.stanford.nlp.trees.TreeAnnotation)) as edu.stanford.nlp.trees.Tree;
            // Find the metaphorical phrase "Life is a journey"
            var journeyPhrase = FindMetaphor(tree);
            // Explain the meaning and significance of the metaphor
            Console.WriteLine("The metaphor in the sentence '{0}' is '{1}'.", sentence, journeyPhrase);
            Console.WriteLine("This metaphor means that life is not just about reaching a destination, but about the journey itself.");
            Console.WriteLine("It suggests that we should focus on the process of personal growth and development, rather than just achieving goals.");
            // Provide examples of how this metaphor can be applied in real-life situations
            Console.WriteLine("For example, instead of just focusing on getting a promotion at work, we should focus on learning new skills and developing our abilities.");
            Console.WriteLine("Similarly, instead of just trying to lose weight, we should focus on developing healthy habits and enjoying the process of becoming more fit.");
        }
        static string FindMetaphor(edu.stanford.nlp.trees.Tree tree)
        {
            // Define the pattern to match the metaphorical phrase "X is Y"
            var pattern = "NP < (NN|NNS $ (VBZ|VBP $ NP < (DT|PRP$ < 'a') < JJ < (NN|NNS)))";
            // Use a TregexMatcher to find the metaphorical phrase in the parse tree
            var matcher = tree.tregex(pattern).matcher();
            while (matcher.find())
            {
                // Get the text of the matched phrase
                var phrase = matcher.getMatch().toString();
                // Replace the "is" with "means"
                phrase = phrase.Replace(" is ", " means ");
                return phrase;
            }
            return "";
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, the `FindMetaphor` method uses the Tregex pattern `"NP < (NN|NNS $ (VBZ|VBP $ NP < (DT|PRP$ < 'a') < JJ < (NN|NNS)))"` to match the metaphorical phrase "X is Y", where X is a noun phrase and Y is an adjective-noun phrase. 
The `Main` method calls the `FindMetaphor` method to identify the metaphorical phrase "Life is a journey", and then explains the meaning and significance of the metaphor in the context of personal growth and development. It also provides examples of how this metaphor can be applied in real-life situations. 
Note that you will need to download and reference the Stanford CoreNLP library in your C# project in order to use this code.

The formula to calculate the minimum score required to pass a test with 15 questions and a passing score of 80%, given that each question has a different weightage, can be expressed in LaTeX format as:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{15} w_i x_i}{\sum_{i=1}^{15} w_i} \geq 0.8
\end{equation}
where:
- $w_i$ represents the weightage of the $i$-th question
- $x_i$ represents the score obtained by the student in the $i$-th question
To calculate the minimum score required to pass the test, we need to solve for $\sum_{i=1}^{15} w_i x_i$:
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i=1}^{15} w_i x_i \geq 0.8 \sum_{i=1}^{15} w_i
\end{equation}
So, the minimum score required to pass the test is:
\begin{equation}
\frac{0.8 \sum_{i=1}^{15} w_i}{\sum_{i=1}^{15} w_i} = 0.8
\end{equation}
Therefore, the minimum score required to pass the test is 80%.

