Here is an example code that demonstrates how to classify the given text using NLP techniques with JavaScript:
```javascript
const natural = require('natural');
const classifier = new natural.BayesClassifier();
// Preprocessing the text
const text = "The stock market went up today";
const tokenizer = new natural.WordTokenizer();
const tokens = tokenizer.tokenize(text.toLowerCase());
const stemmer = natural.PorterStemmer;
const stemmedTokens = tokens.map(token => stemmer.stem(token));
// Extracting the relevant features
const TfIdf = natural.TfIdf;
const tfidf = new TfIdf();
tfidf.addDocument(stemmedTokens);
const wordScores = {};
tfidf.listTerms(0).forEach(item => {
  wordScores[item.term] = item.tfidf;
});
// Sentiment analysis
const SentimentAnalyzer = natural.SentimentAnalyzer;
const stemmer = natural.PorterStemmer;
const analyzer = new SentimentAnalyzer('English', stemmer, 'afinn');
const sentiment = analyzer.getSentiment(tokens);
// Training the classifier
classifier.addDocument(stemmedTokens, sentiment);
// Applying the selected algorithm for classification
classifier.train();
const category = classifier.classify(stemmedTokens);
// Output the result
console.log(`The text "${text}" is classified as ${category}`);
```
In this example, we first preprocess the text by converting it to lowercase, tokenizing it, and stemming the tokens. Then, we extract the relevant features using TF-IDF. We also perform sentiment analysis on the text using the AFINN lexicon. We then train a Naive Bayes classifier with the preprocessed text and sentiment as the label. Finally, we apply the trained classifier to classify the text into one of the three categories: bullish, bearish, or neutral.

