To add a dynamic feature to the drop-down list in R Shiny app using selectInput function, you can use the observeEvent function to detect when the "red" option is selected, and then use the updateBackgroundColor function to change the background color of the app interface to red. Here's an example code:
```
library(shiny)
ui <- fluidPage(
  selectInput("color", "Select a color:",
              choices = c("red" = "Red", "blue" = "Blue", "green" = "Green", "yellow" = "Yellow"),
              selected = "Red"),
  textOutput("selected_color")
)
server <- function(input, output, session) {
  observeEvent(input$color, {
    if(input$color == "Red") {
      updateBackgroundColor(session, "body", "red")
    } else {
      updateBackgroundColor(session, "body", "white")
    }
  })

  output$selected_color <- renderText({
    paste("You selected:", input$color)
  })
}
shinyApp(ui, server)
```
In this code, the observeEvent function is used to detect when the "color" input changes. If the selected option is "Red", the updateBackgroundColor function is used to change the background color of the app interface to red. Otherwise, the background color is set to white. The output$selected_color function simply displays the selected color to the user.

